From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Erik Bourget <erik@ebourget.net>
Date: Thu, 5 Nov 2026 00:08:49 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] thunderbolt: re-link the host DP tunnel left alone at HBR3

Under TB_HOST_DP_SHARED_RATE two DP tunnels from the host router run at
HBR2. When one of them goes because its display or its router was
unplugged, the one left stays at HBR2 although it could now run at HBR3.

Set it up again at up to HBR3, between the same adapters, once a
debounce of 2.5 s is over, from the re-link work on the domain's ordered
workqueue (so that DPRX or teardown work queued before it has released
its paths):

- only after a hot unplug of a DP OUT adapter or a router that took DP
  tunnels from the host router, and only for the one left alone below the
  rate both its adapters take. DPRX failures, failed activations and
  teardown for system sleep never lead to it;
- once per unplug: the tunnel set up again is not marked;
- dropped when another DP tunnel is up or can be paired by then, so an
  unplug and replug, or a display that drops and asserts HPD again while
  it retrains, causes no re-link. Until it is set up again the tunnel
  keeps its HBR2 cap and is counted at HBR2. When another one is wanted
  during the debounce, the tunnel is held at HBR2, its estimated
  bandwidth is worked out again and the debounce is stopped, all before
  the new one is given any bandwidth, so the new one gets HBR2 x4 next
  to it;
- when a second DP tunnel takes the rate back down within a minute of
  such a re-link, the next debounce is doubled, up to 40 s;
- not during a system sleep transition: tunnels that go then mark
  nothing, and a re-link that is due waits for the transition to end; a
  marked tunnel is looked at again at resume and runtime resume;
- if the tunnel cannot be set up at the higher rate, it is set up at
  HBR2 again at once; if it is set up but does not come up (DPRX), it is
  set up at HBR2 again once its DPRX work is over, with its adapters kept
  for pairing and no new re-link up. If neither works, pairing runs again,
  at HBR2, and at the next event.

The KUnit tests run the marking and the pairing on such a domain too.
The DP repair host test models the connection manager: add the new
fields there, with no tunnel re-linked at a higher rate.

Signed-off-by: Erik Bourget <erik@ebourget.net>

(backported from commit d279799da84554923af7b643ef7aa5f5dc35db2b)
[j604: this tree's tb_dp_tunnel_active() takes the tunnel only (no
 data pointer): the tb_cm lookup goes through tunnel->tb; the host test's
 tunnel model keeps its one-argument callback and gains host_relinked_up.]
Signed-off-by: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5.5
